course: dictionary
lesson: 8924
title: Sifting for meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To sift means to shake something through a sieve or fine net, so that the small bits fall through and the lumps or big bits stay behind. The word can also mean to look carefully through many things to find what is useful.
explain: «Sunshine sifting through the leaves» paints a picture. Light falls through the gaps in the leaves, a bit like flour falling through a sieve. The clues in the sentence show that sifting here means something gentle and light, not a hard shake.
explain: When a detective sifts through clues, she looks at each one carefully and keeps only the ones that help her solve the case. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: "She searched carefully through the clues" still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: To sift means to shake something through a sieve, or to look carefully through many things.
fact: Sifting flour breaks up the lumps so the flour is smooth.
fact: Sifting can separate small things from big things, such as sand from stones.
fact: Grey whales sift small creatures out of the mud on the seabed.
fact: A bird with a flat beak can sift food from water.
exercise: «Grey whales feed by sucking mud from the seabed and sifting out small creatures.» What does sifting out mean here? | To pour all the mud and water away | To swim away quickly from the seabed | To shake the seabed into dust | *To separate and catch the small creatures from the mud
exercise: «A flat beak sifts food from water.» What does sifts mean? | *Strains small food out of water | Bites hard into rocks | Drinks the whole lake dry | Flies over the lake
exercise: Which word in «sunshine sifting through the leaves» is the verb? | *sifting | through | leaves | sunshine
test: Detectives sift through clues. What does sift through mean here? | To throw the clues away without looking at them | To lose the clues in a box | *To look carefully through many things to find what is useful
test: Which job uses sifting in cooking? | Mixing paint on a plate | Boiling eggs in water | Cutting onions into small pieces | *Sifting flour to break up the lumps
exam: Sam says sift is just a fancy word for shake. Is he right? | *No, sifting means shaking through a sieve to separate, or looking carefully through things | Yes, sift and shake mean exactly the same | Yes, sift only means shake very hard
